    {
      "design": "r1B",
      "good": "Name and Required are visible together at first, so readers can scan identifiers alongside their status. The overall table is narrower than r1A, reducing horizontal travel.",
      "problems": "Several identifiers visibly break across lines, sometimes inside a word, making exact names harder to read and copy. Tall rows still leave substantial empty space, and Default and Description require scrolling.",
      "cause": "The 260px Name column provides only 239px of text space, which is too narrow for several identifiers. The 220px Description column produces extensive wrapping and dominates row height."
    },
    {
      "design": "r1C",
      "good": "Identifiers and compact values have room to remain intact. The wider Description column gives prose a more comfortable measure and produces a much tighter vertical rhythm than r1A, with fewer large blank areas.",
      "problems": "The initial view contains only names, and the longest names extend past the viewport. Readers must scroll a long way to reach Description, despite its contribution to the improved row rhythm.",
      "cause": "The 398px Name column exceeds the viewport, while the 360px Description column brings the total width to 1048px."
    }
